ns.\n4. Normality of the residuals.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\n- **Resolution IV** is often selected when sample size is a constraint, as it requires fewer runs compared to Resolution V designs. It is suitable for exploratory studies where the primary interest is in estimating main effects.\n- **Resolution V** is preferred when the researcher aims to estimate both main effects and two-factor interactions without confounding, and when sufficient resources (sample size) are available.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of both designs typically involves:\n1. Conducting ANOVA to assess the significance of main effects and interactions.\n2. Using regression analysis to estimate the coefficients of the model.\n3. Checking model assumptions through residual analysis.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- **Resolution IV:** More economical in terms of sample size, allowing for the exploration of a larger number of factors with fewer experimental runs.\n- **Resolution V:** Provides a clearer understanding of interactions by allowing estimation of two-factor interactions, leading to more robust conclusions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- **Resolution IV:** The potential for confounding two-factor interactions may lead to misinterpretation of results if significant interactions are present.\n- **Resolution V:** Requires a larger sample size, which may not be feasible in all experimental contexts.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nOther related designs include:\n- Full factorial designs, which do not confound any effects but require a larger number of runs.\n- Higher resolution designs (e.g., Resolution VI) that further reduce confounding but require even more resources.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nResearchers should consider the trade-off between the resolution of the design and the available resources. The choice of resolution should align with the objectives of the study and the complexity of